\\\"wave-map-energy\\\",\\\"spec_sha256\\\":\\\"controller-bound\\\",\\\"propositions\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H001\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For all x∈R^n and r>0, |u(x,r)|=1.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H002\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For all x∈R^n,r>0 there exists a∈R such that u_tt(x,r)-Δ_xu(x,r)=a u(x,r), with componentwise Δ_x.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"Δ_x\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H003\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For each compact interval J⊂(0,∞), there is compact C_J⊂R^n such that u(x,r)=q for x∉C_J and r∈J.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"q\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"P\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"target\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every r>0, e(·,r) is Lebesgue integrable, E is differentiable at r, and E'(r)=0.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D001\\\",\\\"D002\\\",\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"e\\\",\\\"E\\\",\\\"u\\\"]}],\\\"inferences\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R001\\\",\\\"inputs\\\":[\\\"H001\\\",\\\"H002\\\",\\\"H003\\\"],\\\"output\\\":\\\"P\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D001\\\",\\\"D002\\\",\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"rules\\\":[\\\"direct\\\",\\\"forall_introduction\\\"],\\\"locals\\\":[{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"r0\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"positive real number\\\",\\\"scope\\\":\\\"R001/argument\\\",\\\"start\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"end\\\":\\\"S004\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"arbitrary\\\",\\\"declaration\\\":\\\"Fix arbitrary r0>0.\\\",\\\"from_inputs\\\":[],\\\"from_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[]},{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"J\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"compact interval in (0,∞) with r0 in its interior\\\",\\\"scope\\\":\\\"R001/argument\\\",\\\"start\\\":\\\"S002\\\",\\\"end\\\":\\\"S004\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"witness\\\",\\\"declaration\\\":\\\"Choose J using the earlier existence fact F001.\\\",\\\"from_inputs\\\":[],\\\"from_local\\\":[\\\"F001\\\"],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r0\\\"]},{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"C_J\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"compact subset of R^n\\\",\\\"scope\\\":\\\"R001/argument\\\",\\\"start\\\":\\\"S002\\\",\\\"end\\\":\\\"S004\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"witness\\\",\\\"declaration\\\":\\\"Choose the compact set supplied by H003 for J.\\\",\\\"from_inputs\\\":[\\\"H003\\\"],\\\"from_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"J\\\"]}],\\\"argument\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Fix arbitrary r0>0. Choose δ with 0<δ<r0 and set J=[r0−δ,r0+δ]; hence a compact interval J⊂(0,∞) with r0 in its interior exists. Record this existence before choosing J as F001.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[],\\\"uses_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r0\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"F001\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"There exists a compact interval J⊂(0,∞) with r0 in its interior.\\\",\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r0\\\"],\\\"assumptions\\\":[]}],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S002\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Choose J by F001 and apply H003 to obtain compact C_J with u=q outside C_J at every time in J. For x outside C_J, u is constant on a spatial neighborhood, and constant in time on J; its spatial and time first derivatives vanish in the interior time interval. By continuity they vanish on boundary points as well. Thus e(·,s) is continuous, zero outside C_J and bounded on C_J for each s∈J, hence Lebesgue integrable. The compact C_J has finite Lebesgue measure. Since e_s is continuous on C_J×J, it is uniformly continuous there. For small h with r0+h∈J, FTC gives [e(x,r0+h)−e(x,r0)]/h=∫_0^1 e_s(x,r0+θh)dθ. Uniform continuity makes these quotients converge uniformly on C_J to e_s(x,r0), and finite measure implies convergence of their integrals. Since E(s)=∫_{C_J}e(x,s)dx, E'(r0)=∫_{C_J}e_s(x,r0)dx. Differentiating the finite sum yields e_s=2u_t·u_tt+2Σ_j u_j·∂_j u_t, where u_j=∂_j u.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[\\\"H003\\\"],\\\"uses_local\\\":[\\\"F001\\\"],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"J\\\",\\\"C_J\\\",\\\"u\\\",\\\"q\\\",\\\"e\\\",\\\"E\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S003\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"For each j, f_j(x)=u_t(x,r0)·u_j(x,r0) is smooth and compactly supported. Its derivative is (∂_j u_t)·u_j+u_t·u_jj, continuous with compact support and therefore absolutely integrable. Integrate first in x_j on a finite interval containing the support: the one-dimensional FTC gives zero because f_j vanishes at both endpoints. Fubini applies to this continuous compactly supported derivative, so its integral over R^n is zero. Thus ∫(∂_j u_t)·u_j=−∫u_t·u_jj. Summing yields E'(r0)=2∫u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu). Differentiating |u|²=1 in time gives u·u_t=0. H002 gives u_tt−Δ_xu=a u pointwise, so u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu)=0. Therefore E'(r0)=0.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[\\\"H001\\\",\\\"H002\\\"],\\\"uses_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"u_t\\\",\\\"u_j\\\",\\\"u_jj\\\",\\\"Δ_x\\\",\\\"a\\\",\\\"E\\\",\\\"n\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S004\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"As r0>0 was arbitrary, the conclusions hold for every positive time; no endpoint assertion is used.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[],\\\"uses_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r0\\\",\\\"e\\\",\\\"E\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]}],\\\"applications\\\":[]}]}}\"}","status":"completed"}}

